        </div>

        <!-- Women's Panel -->
        <div id="panel-women" class="table-panel" role="tabpanel" style="display: none;">
          <h3 class="table-heading">Women's UK to US Shoe Size Chart</h3>
          <div class="table-wrapper">
            <table class="size-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>UK Size</th>
                  <th>US Women Size</th>
                  <th>EU Size</th>
                  <th>Foot Length (CM)</th>
                  <th>Inches</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {womenSizes.map(r => (
                  <tr>
                    <td class="primary-cell"><strong>UK {r.uk}</strong></td>
                    <td class="highlight-cell"><strong>US {r.us}</strong></td>
                    <td>EU {r.eu}</td>
                    <td>{r.cm} cm</td>
                    <td>{r.inch}″</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Kids' Panel -->
        <div id="panel-kids" class="table-panel" role="tabpanel" style="display: none;">
          <h3 class="table-heading">Kids' UK to US Shoe Size Chart</h3>
          <div class="table-wrapper">
            <table class="size-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>UK Kids Size</th>
                  <th>US Kids Size</th>
                  <th>EU Size</th>
                  <th>Footbed (CM)</th>
                  <th>Development Stage</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {kidsSizes.map(r => (
                  <tr>
                    <td class="primary-cell"><strong>UK {r.uk}</strong></td>
                    <td class="highlight-cell"><strong>US {r.us}</strong></td>
                    <td>EU {r.eu}</td>
                    <td>{r.cm} cm</td>
                    <td>{r.stage}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Editorial Guide -->
    <section class="editorial-band">
      <div class="page-inner prose-article">
        <h2 class="editorial-main-title">The Complete UK to US Shoe Size Guide: Conversion Math, Lasts &amp; Brand Nuances</h2>

        <p class="lead-paragraph">
          If you are shopping for iconic British footwear—from Dr. Martens boots and Clarks desert shoes to Goodyear-welted oxfords from Northampton makers like Tricker's and Loake—knowing how to <strong>convert uk shoe size to us</strong> is vital. Because both systems share the imperial barleycorn system, understanding the baseline difference allows you to find your perfect American size with confidence.
        </p>

        <h3>1. The Geometry of the British Barleycorn Scale</h3>
        <p>
          Shoe sizing in both the United Kingdom and North America traces back to King Edward II's decree in 1324 establishing the <strong>barleycorn</strong> (one-third of an inch, or 8.46 millimeters) as standard length unit. When applied to footwear lasts:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>A full size step equals <strong>1/3 inch (8.46 mm)</strong>.</li>
          <li>A half-size step equals <strong>1/6 inch (4.23 mm)</strong>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Because both countries utilize this identical stepping increment, conversions do not fluctuate randomly. Instead, they shift by fixed numerical offsets created by where each national standard placed its size 1 starting mark.
        </p>

        <h3>2. Men's Conversion: Adding 0.5 to Your British Size (`US = UK + 0.5`)</h3>
        <p>
          For men, <strong>convert uk to us shoe size</strong> by adding 0.5 to your British size. Whether you are looking up a <strong>uk sneaker size to us</strong> or shopping for formal oxfords, this half-size rule remains consistent:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>UK size 6 in us</strong> (or <strong>uk 6 in us size shoes</strong>) = US Men's 6.5 (approx. 24.5 cm)</li>
          <li><strong>UK size 7 in us</strong> (or <strong>uk 7 shoes to us</strong>) = US Men's 7.5 (approx. 25.5 cm)</li>
          <li><strong>UK 8 to us</strong> (including <strong>uk 8 size to us</strong>, <strong>uk 8 is us size</strong>, and <strong>uk size 8 in us shoes</strong>) = US Men's 8.5 (approx. 26.5 cm)</li>
          <li><strong>UK 9 to us</strong> (including <strong>uk size 9 in us</strong> and <strong>uk 9 shoe size to us</strong>) = US Men's 9.5 (approx. 27.5 cm)</li>
          <li><strong>UK 9.5 to us</strong> = US Men's 10.0 (approx. 28.0 cm)</li>
          <li><strong>UK size 10 in us</strong> (including <strong>uk size 10 in us size</strong>, <strong>uk size ten in us</strong>, and <strong>uk 10 in us size shoes</strong>) = US Men's 10.5 (approx. 28.5 cm)</li>
          <li><strong>UK 12 to us size</strong> = US Men's 12.5–13.0 (approx. 31.0 cm)</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          This half-size adjustment applies directly across major athletic sneaker brands (such as Nike, New Balance, and Asics) and British heritage footwear.
        </p>

        <h3>3. Women's Conversion: Adding 2 Full Sizes (`US = UK + 2`)</h3>
        <p>
          In women's footwear, the scaling gap widens. In the United Kingdom, women's shoe sizes share the same baseline numbering as men's sizes. In the United States, women's sizing starts ahead of the men's scale by approximately 1.5 to 2 sizes.
        </p>
        <p>
          Therefore, for <strong>uk shoe size to us women</strong> or <strong>uk women's shoe size to us</strong>, add 2 full sizes:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>UK size 3 in us</strong> = US Women's 5.0 (approx. 22.0 cm)</li>
          <li><strong>UK size 4 in us</strong> (or <strong>4 uk size in us</strong> and <strong>uk 4 in us size shoes</strong>) = US Women's 6.0 (approx. 23.0 cm)</li>
          <li><strong>UK size 5 in us</strong> (or <strong>uk 5 to us shoe size</strong>) = US Women's 7.0 (approx. 24.0 cm)</li>
          <li><strong>UK size 6 in us shoes</strong> = US Women's 8.0 (approx. 25.0 cm)</li>
          <li><strong>UK size 7 in us</strong> = US Women's 9.0 (approx. 26.0 cm)</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          This two-size rule ensures accurate heel grip and arch support when importing British women's Chelsea boots, flats, and court shoes.
        </p>

        <h3>4. Boot and Heritage Sizing: Dr. Martens, Clarks, and Chelsea Boots</h3>
        <p>
          When shopping for <strong>uk boot size to us</strong> or <strong>uk to us boot size</strong>, keep in mind that classic British bootmakers often manufacture exclusively in full sizes without half sizes:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Dr. Martens (AirWair):</strong> Authentic Docs are sized in UK whole numbers (e.g., UK 7, UK 8, UK 9). Because Docs leather is thick and requires a break-in period, US half-size wearers (such as a US Men's 9.5) should typically choose the UK 9 rather than sizing down to an 8, and use an aftermarket insole for a snug custom fit.</li>
          <li><strong>Clarks Originals:</strong> Desert Boots and Wallabees use UK lasts that offer generous forefoot volume. If you wear an American US 10 Men, a UK 9.5 fits true to length.</li>
          <li><strong>Goodyear-Welted Dress Shoes:</strong> British formal shoes (such as Church's or Loake) default to an 'F' width fitting (comparable to American 'D' medium). If you have wide feet ('EE' in the US), search for British 'G' or 'H' fittings.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>5. Verifying Sizing via Centimetres (CM) and Mondopoint</h3>
        <p>
          To avoid costly international returns when shopping cross-Atlantic, measuring your <strong>feet size uk to us</strong> in centimeters provides an objective, foolproof baseline. Both American and British shoeboxes typically print the Japanese (JP) or Mondopoint metric measurement, which directly reflects the anatomical length of your foot in centimeters or millimeters.
        </p>
        <p>
          Unlike numerical sizes that shift based on national tradition, a 27.0 cm footbed is universally identical whether marked as a British UK 8.5 or an American US 9.0. Checking your metric footbed length guarantees that athletic runners, sneakers, and slip-on loafers provide the necessary 10–12 mm of wiggle room in front of your toes.
        </p>

        <h3>6. Practical Cross-Border Checklist for British Footwear Buyers</h3>
        <p>
          When placing orders for British shoes or boots from the United States or international retailers, follow this quick checklist:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Identify the Native Last System:</strong> Determine whether the brand is using its native British sizing scale or has already pre-converted the size label for US retail. For example, UK-headquartered retailers like ASOS, END., and Selfridges list primary sizes in UK numbers unless you toggle the US currency/region filter.</li>
          <li><strong>Consider Sock Thickness for Boots:</strong> Heavy Goodyear-welted leather boots and commando-sole brogues are often paired with dense wool or hiking socks. If you are between sizes, round up to the next half-size to accommodate extra volume.</li>
          <li><strong>Width Lettering Differences:</strong> Remember that standard medium in British shoes is labeled 'F', while standard medium in American footwear is 'D'. If you require wide footwear, select a British 'G' or 'H' fitting.</li>
          <li><strong>Consult the Category-Specific Chart:</strong> Always reference our dedicated <strong>shoe size guide uk to us</strong> tables above for men, women, and children to ensure you never confuse women's 2-size offsets with men's half-size offsets.</li>
